    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Okinawan civilian dead are counted differently by different bodies: at least 149,425 killed, dead by coerced suicide or missing by the local authorities' count, and about 42,000 non-uniformed civilians by the US Tenth Army's, and the figures are not reconciled.</li><li>The Japanese Thirty-Second Army fought a defensive battle from fortified caves and reverse slopes, and for the first time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a> attacks became a major institutionalised part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s defence.</li><li>Civilians were used as human shields, had food taken from them, and were urged to kill themselves and their families; the extent of the army's role in the mass suicides is still disputed between Okinawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s national government.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Japanese army mobilised 1,780 schoolboys aged 14 to 17 into front-line service, and female students into a nursing unit; about half of the boys were killed.</p></aside>
    </section>

