    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">March 1945</span><span class="rf-text">The US 77th Infantry Division takes the Kerama Islands west of Okinawa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1945</span><span class="rf-text">The Tenth Army lands on the Hagushi beaches and takes the Kadena and Yomitan airbases within hours</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1945</span><span class="rf-text">About 400 Japanese planes attack from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> on 6 April, and the battleship Yamato sails on a suicide attack and is sunk</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1945</span><span class="rf-text">The Thirty-Second Army attacks across the whole front on three nights and is repulsed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1945</span><span class="rf-text">The 96th Infantry Division takes Conical Hill, and the Marines fight for Sugar Loaf Hill</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1945</span><span class="rf-text">Marines occupy Shuri Castle after the Japanese withdraw south</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1945</span><span class="rf-text">General Buckner is killed by Japanese artillery fire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1945</span><span class="rf-text">The last Japanese resistance ends and Ushijima and Chō die by suicide</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-okinawa" data-v="d8d0574fd2" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Kerama Islands are taken</b> Soldiers of the 77th Infantry Division landed in the Kerama Islands, 15 miles west of Okinawa. Task Force 58 lay to the east of the island, the Thirty-Second Army held the south and Udo Force the north.</li><li><b>The main landing at the Hagushi beaches</b> The XXIV Corps and the III Amphibious Corps landed on the Hagushi beaches on the west coast. Off the Minatoga beaches in the south-east the 2nd Marine Division made a demonstration to deceive the Japanese.</li><li><b>The Marines close the north and the 77th takes Ie Shima</b> The 6th Marine Division sealed off the Motobu Peninsula, where Udo Force was cornered around Mount Yaedake, and cleared it on 18 April. The 77th Division assaulted Ie Shima on 16 April.</li><li><b>The advance south meets Kakazu Ridge</b> The 96th and 7th Infantry Divisions wheeled south and met Japanese soldiers in fortified caves at Kakazu Ridge, part of Shuri's outer defences. The advance went on, with heavy casualties on both sides.</li><li><b>Ushijima attacks across the front</b> Ushijima, influenced by General Chō, took the offensive and the Thirty-Second Army attacked American positions across the whole front on the evening of 12 April. The assaults of the following nights were repulsed.</li><li><b>The offensive of 19 April meets the Japanese defences</b> General Hodge launched a new offensive with a barrage of 324 guns, joined by ships and by 650 aircraft. The Japanese waited on reverse slopes, and a tank assault round Kakazu Ridge failed, so there was no breakthrough.</li><li><b>The Japanese counter-offensive of 4 May fails</b> Ushijima attempted amphibious assaults on the coasts behind the American lines, and his artillery moved into the open to support it. American counter-battery fire destroyed dozens of guns, and the attack failed.</li><li><b>Conical Hill and Sugar Loaf Hill are taken</b> The 96th Infantry Division took Conical Hill, the eastern anchor of the main Japanese defences, while the 1st and 6th Marine Divisions fought for Sugar Loaf Hill on the opposite coast. Their capture exposed the Japanese round Shuri on both sides.</li><li><b>Shuri is left and the army withdraws south</b> The 6th Marine Division occupied the ruins of Naha, and Marines of the 1st Marine Division took Shuri Castle on 29 May, which the Thirty-Second Army had left. The army moved nearly 30,000 men to the Kiyan Peninsula.</li><li><b>The Oroku Peninsula is taken</b> Elements of the 6th Marine Division launched an amphibious assault on the Oroku Peninsula. The 4,000 Japanese sailors, including Admiral Ōta, killed themselves in the tunnels of their underground headquarters on 13 June.</li><li><b>The last pocket in the south</b> By 17 June the remnants of Ushijima’s Thirty-Second Army were pushed into a small pocket in the far south of the island, to the southeast of Itoman.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By early 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had advanced across the Pacific by island hopping. They planned to use Kadena Air Base on Okinawa as a staging point for Operation Downfall, the planned invasion of the Japanese home islands, 340 miles (550 km) away. Okinawa was the largest amphibious assault of the Pacific Theater. Before the main landing, the 77th Infantry Division captured the Kerama Islands on 26 March, which gave the fleet a protected anchorage and removed the threat from suicide boats.</p><p>The Japanese expected the Americans to land six to ten divisions against a garrison of two and a half divisions. Their staff calculated that, with superior weapons, each American division had five or six times the firepower of a Japanese division, to which the Americans would add abundant naval and air firepower. The 9th Division was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> before the invasion, which forced a reshuffle of the defensive plans.</p><p>Within the Thirty-Second Army, Colonel Hiromichi Yahara advocated a defensive strategy and Lieutenant General Isamu Chō an offensive one. The Japanese had used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a> tactics since the Battle of Leyte Gulf, but at Okinawa they became a major institutionalised part of the defence. Okinawa was also home to a large civilian population, which US planners assumed numbered about 300,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Americans formed the Tenth Army, a cross-branch force of the 7th, 27th, 77th and 96th Infantry Divisions and the 1st, 2nd and 6th Marine Divisions, with its own Tactical Air Force. It had 182,821 personnel at the start, under Lieutenant General Simon Bolivar Buckner Jr. The invasion was supported by 18 battleships, 27 cruisers, 177 destroyers and destroyer escorts and 39 aircraft carriers, and over 3,000 aircraft were provided over the battle. A British Commonwealth fleet with Australian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and Canadian ships served as Task Force 57.</p><p>The Japanese land campaign, mainly defensive, was fought by the 32nd Army of 67,000 men (77,000 according to some sources), under Lieutenant General Mitsuru Ushijima, and some 9,000 naval troops at Oroku under Rear Admiral Minoru Ōta, only a few hundred of them trained for ground combat. They were supported by 39,000 drafted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Ryukyuan people</a>: 24,000 hastily drafted militia called Boeitai and 15,000 non-uniformed labourers. Colonel Takehiko Udo commanded in the north.</p><p>The army also mobilised 1,780 schoolboys aged 14 to 17 as the Iron and Blood Imperial Corps, while female Himeyuri students were organised into a nursing unit. The mobilisation was by ordinance of the Ministry of the Army, not by law. The students were nominally volunteers, but the authorities ordered schools to make almost all of them volunteer, sometimes with forged documents. About half of the corps were killed, among the 2,000 students of 21 schools who died on the battlefield.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Okinawa&#8221; cites where it covers Allied:</p><ul><li>Gordon Rottman, Okinawa 1945: The Last Battle (Osprey Publishing, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 28 March, 394 civilians on Tokashiki island were forced by Japanese soldiers to kill themselves after US troops landed. The main landing came on 1 April on the Hagushi beaches on the west coast, and the Tenth Army took the Kadena and Yomitan airbases within hours against weak opposition. The 6th Marine Division sealed off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motobu-peninsula">Motobu Peninsula</a> by 7 April and cleared Mount Yaedake on 18 April, and the 77th Infantry Division took Ie Shima between 16 and 21 April. At sea, about 400 Japanese planes attacked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> on 6 April, and the battleship Yamato and five other warships were sunk on their way to Okinawa.</p><p>In the south the Japanese fought from fortified caves on the Shuri Line, with defences on reverse slopes. The advance on Kakazu Ridge was costly, and the Japanese sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a> out at gunpoint to fetch water and supplies, which led to civilian casualties. The Thirty-Second Army counter-attacked on the nights of 12 to 14 April and again on 4 May, and both attacks failed. On 19 April the Americans fired a barrage of 324 guns, the largest in the Pacific Theater. Conical Hill fell on 13 May, and Sugar Loaf Hill was fought for at the same time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">Monsoon</a> rains turned the field to mud.</p><p>The Japanese withdrew by night from Shuri, and Marines entered the castle on 29 May. Nearly 30,000 personnel reached the Kiyan Peninsula. In June the naval troops at Oroku died by suicide in their tunnels on 13 June. General Buckner was killed on 18 June and organised resistance ended on 21 June, when Ushijima and Chō died by seppuku.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Okinawa&#8221; cites where it covers Allied, Naval battle, British Pacific Fleet, Land battle:</p><ul><li>Max Hastings, Retribution – The Battle for Japan, 1944–45 (Alfred A. Knopf, 2008)</li><li>Gordon Rottman, Okinawa 1945: The Last Battle (Osprey Publishing, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Americans suffered some 48,000 casualties, not counting some 33,000 non-battle casualties, and over 12,000 were killed or missing. At sea, 36 Allied ships were sunk and 368 damaged. The US military estimates that 110,071 Japanese soldiers were killed, a total that includes conscripted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a>. 7,401 Japanese regulars and 3,400 Okinawan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> were captured or surrendered during the battle, and 16,346 in all by later months.</p><p>The civilian losses were of another order. The US Tenth Army counted 142,058 enemy bodies, including civilians pressed into service, and deduced that about 42,000 were non-uniformed civilians killed in the crossfire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture">Okinawa Prefecture</a> estimates over 100,000 losses, and the local authorities say at least 149,425 Okinawans were killed, died by coerced suicide or went missing. 40,000 of those killed had been drafted or impressed by the Japanese army and are often counted as combat deaths.</p><p>The Okinawa Prefectural Peace Memorial Museum says the Japanese army showed indifference to Okinawans' safety, used civilians as human shields or killed them, confiscated food and executed those who hid it, leading to mass starvation, and forced civilians out of shelters. Japanese soldiers killed about 1,000 people who spoke the Okinawan language, to suppress spying. Civilians in the south also killed their families and themselves, urged on by soldiers who said the Americans would kill and rape them. At least 90% of the buildings on the island were destroyed.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Okinawa&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Military losses, Civilian losses, suicides, and atrocities, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Michael S. Molasky, The American Occupation of Japan and Okinawa: Literature and Memory (Routledge, 1999)</li><li>Michael S. Molasky, Southern Exposure: Modern Japanese Literature from Okinawa (University of Hawaii Press, 2000)</li><li>Susan D Sheehan, Islands of Discontent: Okinawan Responses to Japanese and American Power (Rowman &amp; Littlefield Publishers, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The most complete tally of deaths is the Cornerstone of Peace monument at the Okinawa Prefectural Peace Memorial Museum, which lists each person by name: 242,046 names as of 2023, including 149,634 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a>, 77,823 Imperial Japanese soldiers and 14,010 Americans. It also counts Okinawan deaths over a longer period than the battle, from the Manchurian Incident to a year after the surrender, so its totals cannot be read as the battle's alone.</p><p>Official counts and local ones differ, and the Okinawan civilian toll depends on whether conscripted Okinawans are counted as civilians or as combatants. Early claims of Japanese aircraft lost, 7,800, were not borne out by later examination of Japanese records, which put the loss at roughly 1,430. The Ryūkyū Shimpō wrote in 2007 that many Okinawans testified that the Japanese army directed them to commit suicide, and some that they were handed grenades. Colonel Yahara, the most senior officer to survive, wrote The Battle for Okinawa.</p><p>Witnesses and historians claim that American and Japanese soldiers raped Okinawan women. Marine Corps officials said they knew of no rapes by American personnel, but there are numerous credible testimonies that many were committed by Americans.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki.jpg" alt="Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atomic_bombing_of_Japan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George R. Caron / Charles Levy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historians have judged the battle differently. Hanson W. Baldwin wrote that never before had the Navy lost so many ships, and that the American casualties were the highest in any campaign against the Japanese. Some military historians, among them Victor Davis Hanson, believe the campaign led directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a>, as a means of avoiding an invasion of the Japanese mainland. Others debate why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> surrendered, citing the bombings, the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and depleted resources. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> then set up the United States Civil Administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>, a military government.</p><p>The civilian suicides remain disputed. In March 2007 Japan's education ministry (MEXT) advised textbook publishers to reword descriptions saying that the army forced civilians to kill themselves. On 29 September 2007 about 110,000 people rallied in Okinawa in protest. In December 2007 the ministry's council allowed the wording that civilians were forced into mass suicides by the military, with context. In March 2008 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> court ruled for Kenzaburō Ōe, who had been sued over his statement that the military ordered them, saying the military was deeply involved.</p><p>In 1995 the Okinawa government erected the Cornerstone of Peace at Mabuni, which lists all the known dead, civilian and military, Japanese and foreign. It listed 242,225 names as of 2024. Kadena remains the largest US air base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and local residents have long protested against the size and presence of the bases.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Battle of Okinawa, from 1 April to 22 June 1945, was the largest amphibious assault of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>. The Americans took the island, the Japanese Thirty-Second Army was destroyed, and at least 149,425 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a> were killed, died by coerced suicide or went missing.</em></p>

